# cursor-cloud

## cli
- label: tracequest gains an explicit async `import` subcommand: bin/tracequest.js routes case "import" to cmdImport in src/cli/cli-commands.js (lazy-loading the importer from src/import/cursor-cloud-import.js like cmdShare loads share code, and src/import/ssh-import.js for source ssh), "import" joins COMMAND_HELP and the cli-help.js usage text, options are [--dry-run] [--full] [--api-key <key>] [-i/--identity <file>] [--port <N>] [-h/--help], and a missing or unrecognised positional source dies (exit 1, cli-die) with a message naming the supported import sources cursor-cloud and ssh
  id: ccim
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'import with an unknown source dies|import help lists usage and options' test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js && grep -q '"import"' bin/tracequest.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, cli, implemented]
- label: the credential source selects the transport route — with no --api-key flag and no CURSOR_API_KEY environment variable the importer reads this machine's local Cursor session token (fact ccak) and imports over the keyless api2 route (facts cca2, ccah), and only an explicitly supplied --api-key (first) or CURSOR_API_KEY (second) switches it to the documented v0 route (fact ccep); a missing key is never an error while a local token can be read, and only when no explicit key is supplied AND no local token can be read does the importer die (exit 1, cli-die) with an actionable message naming the state.vscdb path it looked at plus both the --api-key flag and the CURSOR_API_KEY environment variable as alternatives
  id: cckey
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'imports keylessly with the local session token|prefers the v0 route when --api-key is given|dies with an actionable message when no credential can be found' test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, cli, implemented]
- label: `tracequest import cursor-cloud --dry-run` performs exactly the same HTTP requests as a real run over whichever route the credential selected (POST connect-rpc calls on the keyless api2 route, GETs on the v0 route) but writes no files and creates no directories under the cursor-cloud root: it prints one planned action per agent (import, update, or skip) plus the final summary, and a subsequent real run starts from an unchanged local state
  id: ccdry
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'dry-run prints planned actions and writes nothing' test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, cli, implemented]
- label: every import run ends with a one-line summary reporting six counts in the order checked, fetched, imported, updated, skipped, failed — checked is the number of distinct agents in the list response, fetched is how many of those actually issued a GetBackgroundComposerConversation (or v0 conversation) request rather than being resolved from the list response alone (fact ccin), and imported/updated/skipped/failed keep their existing meaning and stay contiguous in that order at the END of the line so the incremental win (checked much larger than fetched) is visible at a glance; the process exits 0 when failed is 0 (including the all-skipped idempotent re-run) and exits 1 when any per-agent failure occurred
  id: ccsm
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'summary reports imported, updated, skipped, and failed counts|summary reports checked and fetched counts alongside the write outcomes' test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, cli, implemented]
- label: `tracequest import cursor-cloud --full` is the escape hatch that bypasses the incremental fetch skip of fact ccin entirely — every listed agent's conversation is refetched and re-compared, which is the recovery path when local files are stale, were written by an older importer, or are suspected wrong — while everything downstream is unchanged: the write-skip of fact ccid still applies so an unchanged transcript still leaves the file mtime untouched, the bounded concurrency of fact cccv still applies, --full composes with --dry-run, and the flag is declared in bin/tracequest.js's import parseArgs options, listed in the `import Options:` help block of src/cli/cli-help.js, and documented in the CLI reference import paragraph (fact b8r)
  id: ccfu
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'full refetches every agent including unchanged ones' test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js && sed -n '/case "import"/,/break;/p' bin/tracequest.js | grep -q 'full: { type: "boolean" }' && grep -q -- '--full' src/cli/cli-help.js && grep -q -- '--full' docs/cli-reference.md
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, cli, implemented]
- label: a long import never looks hung: the importer prints the whole incremental plan BEFORE issuing any conversation request — one line naming how many agents were listed, how many are unchanged and will be skipped with no request at all, and how many will be fetched together with the concurrency bound — and every per-agent action line (import, update, skip, or the no-request `skip <id> (unchanged)`) is emitted the moment that agent is decided rather than buffered until the end of the run, so output appears immediately and keeps advancing while the remaining transcripts download
  id: ccpr
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'summary reports checked and fetched counts alongside the write outcomes' test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, cli, implemented]
- label: sessions materialised by `import cursor-cloud` flow through the normal pipeline with no extra flag or rescan command: findSessions discovers them, buildIndex indexes them, and search returns hits whose source is cursor-cloud
  id: ccis
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'imported cursor-cloud sessions are indexed and searchable' test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, cli, implemented]

## transport
- label: api2 requests carry exactly two headers — Content-Type application/json and authorization Bearer with the local session token — and nothing else is required or sent: no cookie, no Origin, no checksum or client-version header; this is the inverse of Cursor's cookie-authenticated web endpoint (a cookie without the bearer header is rejected by api2 with 401), so the importer never constructs a WorkosCursorSessionToken cookie
  id: ccah
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'imports keylessly with the local session token' test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, cli, implemented]
- label: the two credentials belong to two separate auth systems and must never be merged into one code path or one generic token — the local session token is rejected by api.cursor.com v0 with 401 and the message "Invalid User API Key" under both Bearer and Basic, and a dashboard-issued User API Key does not authenticate api2 — so each credential source selects its own route (session token to api2, dashboard key to v0) and neither route is ever retried with the other credential
  id: ccax
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'prefers the v0 route when --api-key is given|imports keylessly with the local session token' test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js && grep -q 'api2.cursor.sh' src/import/cursor-cloud-import.js && grep -q 'api.cursor.com' src/import/cursor-cloud-import.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, cli, implemented]
- label: the ListBackgroundComposers request body is n (page size, 100) plus includeArchived true, and on a follow-up page lastMessageActivityAtMsOffset taken from the previous response's nextPageOffset; the response carries composers plus the optional hasMore and nextPageOffset, and because the backend is proto3 an absent hasMore or nextPageOffset means "no further pages" and ends the walk; the offset boundary is INCLUSIVE so the boundary composer is returned again on the next page — the importer therefore deduplicates by bcId and imports every agent at most once per run
  id: ccpg
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'dedupes the inclusive list pagination boundary by bcId' test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, cli, implemented]
- label: the GetBackgroundComposerConversation request body is the single field bcId carrying the list response's bcId verbatim, and the response is the UNPAGINATED conversation array in chronological order (index 0 is the first human message) — there is no cursor, offset, or continuation field to follow; single responses of several megabytes and several thousand messages are read in full with no truncation and no arbitrary size cap, and the conversations that fact ccin decided actually need fetching are downloaded with BOUNDED concurrency — at most CURSOR_CLOUD_FETCH_CONCURRENCY (4) requests in flight at any instant, pinned as a named constant in src/import/cursor-cloud-import.js — and never with an unbounded fan-out over the whole agent list: the bound is deliberately modest because api2 is an undocumented internal backend that publishes no rate-limit headers and no documented quota, so a small fixed ceiling is the largest speed-up defensible without server cooperation
  id: cccv
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'reads a large single-response conversation without truncation|fetches conversations concurrently within the bound' test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js && grep -q 'CURSOR_CLOUD_FETCH_CONCURRENCY' src/import/cursor-cloud-import.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, cli, implemented]
- label: the importer decides from the LIST response alone whether an agent's transcript needs fetching, so an unchanged agent costs ZERO conversation requests: for every listed agent it first reads the local <root>/<slug>/<agentId>.jsonl first line, and it issues NO GetBackgroundComposerConversation (or v0 conversation) request when that file's session_meta carries a non-empty updatedAt, the remote list entry's updatedAtMs converts to a non-null ISO-8601 string, the two ISO-8601 strings are equal, and createdAt matches too — the comparison is always on the ISO-8601 form written into session_meta and never on raw milliseconds, so it is exactly the representation fact ccid compares; EVERY other case fetches, because correctness beats speed and a skipped fetch must never hide a changed transcript: no local file, an unreadable or corrupt file, a first line that is not session_meta, an absent or empty updatedAt on either side, a differing updatedAt, a differing createdAt, or the --full flag of fact ccfu; an agent resolved this way prints `skip <id> (unchanged)`, counts under skipped, and performs no network I/O and no write at all
  id: ccin
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'issues no conversation request for an unchanged agent|refetches an agent whose remote updatedAtMs changed|fetches an agent that has no local file|refetches when the local file is corrupt or has no session_meta' test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, cli, implemented]
- label: api2 is an undocumented internal backend, so the importer fails SOFT on shape drift instead of asserting the payload: unknown or missing fields anywhere in a composer or conversation message are ignored rather than fatal, a message that cannot be mapped is dropped, an agent whose conversation payload is unusable is counted under failed and the run continues with the remaining agents, and drift in an optional field never aborts the run or crashes the process
  id: ccdf
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'tolerates unknown status, source, and message type values' test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, cli, implemented]
- label: a 404 from either route is skip-with-warning, never fatal — on api2 it is the JSON body with code not_found (a bcId the backend no longer knows) and on v0 it is a 404 from the conversation endpoint (the agent was deleted remotely) — the importer prints a warning naming the agent id to stderr, counts the agent under skipped, continues with the remaining agents, and the exit code is unaffected; any other per-agent error that is neither 404 nor 401 is counted under failed, also without aborting the rest of the run
  id: cc44
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'skips deleted agents with a warning|skips a keyless conversation that 404s with a warning' test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, cli, implemented]
- label: agent ids are used verbatim as returned by the list response — in conversation request bodies, in URLs, and as <agentId>.jsonl filenames — with no format validation or normalisation (the observed bc-<uuid> form is not assumed), and every enum-valued field is tolerated and stored verbatim rather than validated against a closed set: status (BACKGROUND_COMPOSER_STATUS_ERROR, _FINISHED, _RUNNING and any future value), source (SOURCE_GLASS, SOURCE_WEBSITE and any future value), and message type (MESSAGE_TYPE_UNSPECIFIED, _HUMAN, _AI, _SYSTEM and any future value)
  id: ccvb
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'uses agent ids verbatim and tolerates unknown status values|tolerates unknown status, source, and message type values' test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, cli, implemented]
- label: the optional v0 route is the documented Cursor Cloud Agents API, selected only by an explicit dashboard key (fact cckey): GET /v0/agents with limit=100 following nextCursor until the response omits it enumerates agents (id, name, status, source with repository and ref, target, createdAt) and GET /v0/agents/{id}/conversation (treated as unpaginated) returns that agent's chronological messages (id, type user_message or assistant_message, text); v1 is never used because it has no transcript endpoint, and this route is retained unchanged as the documented fallback for when the internal api2 backend drifts
  id: ccep
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'fetches paginated agents and writes jsonl under the cursor-cloud root' test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, cli, implemented]
- label: importing is the only tracequest operation that performs network I/O to Cursor — startup, discovery, indexing, serving, and every other CLI command are unchanged except that discovery stats one additional (possibly absent) cursor-cloud root; because the default route derives its credential from local Cursor state a keyless import still reaches the network, so there is no no-network default and network I/O to Cursor happens exactly when `import cursor-cloud` runs (manual fact; verify by reading the cmdImport wiring in bin/tracequest.js and confirming session-discovery.js only stats the cursor-cloud root, never fetches)
  id: ccni
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, cli, implemented]
- label: the keyless api2 route is the HIGH-fidelity path: transcripts carry per-message ids (bubbleId), assistant reasoning, and full tool-call detail, so imported cursor-cloud sessions populate tools, toolCounts, touched files, and commit counts exactly like local Cursor sessions; the caveats that remain are that api2 messages carry no per-message wall-clock timestamp (real epoch milliseconds exist only inside toolResults startedAtMs/completedAtMs, which the Cursor row shape has nowhere to put) so sessions still record only session_meta timestamps and durationMs (fact ccix), and that the backend is undocumented and may change without notice (fact ccdf); the optional v0 route is the lower-fidelity documented fallback whose messages are text only, with no tool calls and no message-level metadata
  id: ccfy
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'tool results become tool_use rows that produce tools and toolCounts' test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, sessions, implemented]

## credential
- label: the local credential is Cursor's own session token, read read-only from state.vscdb ItemTable key cursorAuth/accessToken (a JWT; the platform db path and the TRACEQUEST_CURSOR_STATE_DB override follow src/sessions/cursor-state-db.js, so the same env var that fixtures model enrichment also fixtures the credential store in tests); the importer reads it lazily at request time and reuses the read-only loader pattern of cursor-state-db.js, never opening the db for writing, and this token is the credential for the api2 route only (fact ccax)
  id: ccak
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'imports keylessly with the local session token' test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js && grep -q 'cursorAuth/accessToken' src/import/cursor-cloud-import.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, cli, implemented]
- label: both credentials (the locally read session token and an explicitly supplied dashboard key) are treated as secrets — neither is ever written into any imported session file (no session_meta and no message row ever contains one), neither is ever printed in normal or --dry-run output (not even truncated or masked with a visible suffix), and neither ever appears in a warning, error, or die message; the credential value is read at request time and passed only into the Authorization header, never into console output, never into the JSONL writer, and never into a thrown error's message
  id: ccse
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'never prints the credential in dry-run or error output' test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, cli, security, implemented]
- label: the local session token expires (the accessToken JWT carries an exp claim and Cursor rotates it), so a 401 from api2 — the JSON body with code unauthenticated and ERROR_NOT_LOGGED_IN, cleanly distinguishable from the not_found body of fact cc44 — makes the importer die (exit 1, cli-die) with an actionable message telling the user to sign in again in Cursor or to pass --api-key / set CURSOR_API_KEY; a 401 is fatal for the whole run rather than a per-agent skip, and the importer never attempts to refresh the token itself
  id: ccxp
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'dies with a re-authenticate message when the local credential is rejected' test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, cli, implemented]
- label: automatically refreshing an expired session token from the cursorAuth/refreshToken sibling key is deliberately out of scope — Cursor's refresh exchange has never been traced or proved, so implementing it would be guesswork; the user re-authenticates in Cursor instead (fact ccxp), and this fact is the placeholder for a future revision that traces the refresh flow first
  id: ccrf
  tags: [draft, cursor-cloud, cli]

## materialisation
- label: imported sessions materialise under the durable tracequest data root ~/.local/share/tracequest/cursor-cloud — deliberately NOT the rebuildable ~/.cache/tracequest index dir, because deleted agents 404 and cannot be re-fetched — resolved by resolveCursorCloudRoot in src/sessions/session-discovery-paths.js as a plain homedir() join (same resolution style as index-writers cacheDir) with the TRACEQUEST_CURSOR_CLOUD_DIR env override (precedent: TRACEQUEST_CURSOR_STATE_DB), and discovery and the importer share that single resolver
  id: ccrt
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'cursor-cloud root is .local/share/tracequest/cursor-cloud under HOME|TRACEQUEST_CURSOR_CLOUD_DIR overrides the cursor-cloud root' test/sessions/discovery-roots.test.js && grep -q 'resolveCursorCloudRoot' src/sessions/session-discovery-paths.js && grep -q 'resolveCursorCloudRoot' src/import/cursor-cloud-import.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, sessions, implemented]
- label: each agent lands at <root>/<project-slug>/<agentId>.jsonl where project-slug is derived from the agent's repository by taking the trailing owner/name and replacing "/" with "-", tolerating a repository value that arrives either with or without a scheme (https://github.com/org/repo and github.com/org/repo both -> org-repo), with the literal fallback slug "cursor-cloud" when the repository is absent, and the file is written atomically (temp file + rename) so discovery never observes a half-written session
  id: ccly
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'fetches paginated agents and writes jsonl under the cursor-cloud root|falls back to the cursor-cloud project slug when repository is absent' test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, sessions, implemented]
- label: line 1 of every imported file is a session_meta row (type session_meta) carrying bcId, name, status, createdAt and the optional fields updatedAt, repository, ref, branchName, prUrl, url, model, with absent fields simply omitted; on the keyless route every value comes from that agent's entry in the LIST response — createdAt and updatedAt are ISO-8601 conversions of createdAtMs and updatedAtMs, status is the string enum stored verbatim, model is modelDetails.modelName, repository is repoUrl verbatim (either observed scheme), and branchName and prUrl are copied when present — while on the v0 route createdAt is the API list value verbatim and updatedAt/model come from the state.vscdb enrichment of fact ccme
  id: ccml
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'writes a session_meta first line with agent metadata|writes session_meta from the keyless list response' test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, sessions, implemented]
- label: every retained conversation message maps to exactly one JSONL line in the Cursor CLI row shape with role as the FIRST key and a message object holding a content array — because isCursorIndexedJsonlLine matches on that literal prefix and cursorIndexedLineKind requires the message object — plus the source bubbleId carried verbatim as message-level provenance; api2 type MESSAGE_TYPE_HUMAN maps to role user and MESSAGE_TYPE_AI to role assistant while MESSAGE_TYPE_SYSTEM, MESSAGE_TYPE_UNSPECIFIED, and any unknown type are dropped rather than guessed, and on the v0 route user_message maps to user and assistant_message to assistant
  id: ccmr
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'maps MESSAGE_TYPE_HUMAN and MESSAGE_TYPE_AI to cursor user and assistant rows|maps v0 user_message and assistant_message rows to the cursor row shape' test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, sessions, implemented]
- label: an api2 message's text and thinking text are emitted as ONE content block of type text, serialized with the type key immediately followed by the text key, holding the message text first and the thinking text appended after a blank line — this is exactly what Cursor's own cursor-agent transcript normaliser writes, it is the only shape both engines index identically (a separate type thinking block is indexed by the JS assistant accumulator but ignored by the Rust CursorRowAccumulator, and its characters would also be missing from the Cursor char-based token estimate, which counts only text blocks), and text-first ordering keeps the answer inside parseCursor's 500-character text cap
  id: cctx
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'maps MESSAGE_TYPE_HUMAN and MESSAGE_TYPE_AI to cursor user and assistant rows' test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, sessions, implemented]
- label: re-running import is idempotent: when the existing file's session_meta has the same createdAt, the same optional updatedAt (compared as the ISO-8601 strings written into session_meta — on the keyless route that is the conversion of the list response's updatedAtMs, so a change of the remote millisecond value is what triggers a rewrite — with both-absent comparing equal), and the file holds the same number of retained message lines as the freshly fetched conversation, the importer skips the write entirely — the file mtime is unchanged, strict mtime-equality staleness means buildIndex does not re-index the session, and because session identity is the agentId (a stable path) a re-import can never duplicate a session; this write-skip composes with the fetch-skip of fact ccin without contradicting it — the message-line-count half of the comparison applies ONLY to agents whose conversation was actually fetched (an agent skipped by ccin has no fetched conversation to count and is never written either), so both skips converge on the same observable outcome of an untouched file, and the write-skip remains the safety net that catches an agent that was fetched but turned out identical
  id: ccid
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 're-run skips unchanged sessions and preserves mtime|full refetches every agent including unchanged ones' test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, sessions, implemented]
- label: state.vscdb enrichment of session_meta model and updatedAt from ItemTable key cloudAgentRepository.agents.<authUser> (matching the JSON array row whose bcId equals the agent id and reading modelDetails.modelName, honouring TRACEQUEST_CURSOR_STATE_DB and reusing the src/sessions/cursor-state-db.js loader pattern) applies to the v0 route ONLY, because the keyless list response already returns modelDetails, createdAtMs, and updatedAtMs directly (fact ccml); on the v0 route it stays fail-soft — any failure (missing DB, missing key, missing row) silently omits the fields and never fails or delays the import — and it is never consulted on the keyless route
  id: ccme
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern '\-\-api-key enriches model from state.vscdb and omits model on failure' test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, sessions, implemented]

## source
- label: cursor-cloud is the seventh known session source: INDEX_SESSION_BY_SOURCE in src/sessions/session-index-core.js maps "cursor-cloud" to indexCursorCloudJsonl so the derived KNOWN_SESSION_SOURCES includes it, and the fail-closed contract of fact 80i is unchanged for genuinely unknown sources (Rust twin: fact ccr7 in sidecar.facts)
  id: ccsv
  command: grep -q '"cursor-cloud"' src/sessions/session-index-core.js && node -e "import('./src/sessions/session-index-core.js').then(m=>{if(!m.KNOWN_SESSION_SOURCES.includes('cursor-cloud'))process.exit(1)})"
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, sessions, implemented]
- label: getDiscoveryPaths gains cursorCloudDir (the resolveCursorCloudRoot result) ordered directly after cursorDir, discoveryRoots returns seven roots with openCodeDb still last, and cursor-cloud discovery reuses the Claude-family walker over <root>/<project-slug>/<agentId>.jsonl emitting rows with source=cursor-cloud, project=slug, size, and mtime; an absent root is silently skipped like every other source (fact 15z)
  id: ccdw
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'returns exactly seven provider roots|discovers cursor-cloud jsonl under the cursor-cloud root' test/sessions/discovery-roots.test.js test/sessions/session-discovery.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, sessions, implemented]
- label: indexCursorCloudJsonl reuses indexCursorJsonl's Claude-family machinery for message rows (factory-style token estimation per fact vkj, Cursor `path` file tracking) with three session_meta deltas: model comes from session_meta.model before the "cursor-cloud" fallback, durationMs = max(0, updatedAt - createdAt) from the meta line (0 when updatedAt is absent) instead of cursorFileTimeBounds, and firstPrompt falls back to session_meta.name when the transcript has no user message (Rust twin: fact ccrx in sidecar.facts)
  id: ccix
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'cursor-cloud session_meta supplies real timestamps and durationMs|cursor-cloud indexer reuses cursor message-row indexing' test/sessions/session-index-cursor-cloud.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, sessions, implemented]
- label: cursor-cloud sessions whose session_meta carries no model fall back to the model literal "cursor-cloud" consistently in indexCursorCloudJsonl, peekCursorCloud, parseCursorCloud, and Rust index_cursor_cloud (mirror of local-cursor fact im6's 'cursor' fallback), so cloud sessions with unknown model are never conflated with local cursor sessions in model filters and stats
  id: ccmf
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'cursor-cloud indexer falls back to model cursor-cloud when meta has no model' test/sessions/session-index-cursor-cloud.test.js && grep -q '"cursor-cloud"' sidecar/src/indexer.rs
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, sessions, implemented]
- label: parseSession auto-detects files under the cursor-cloud root and parseSession(path, "cursor-cloud") preserves source=cursor-cloud; parseCursorCloud reuses parseCursor's event semantics, sets sessionId to the file's basename agentId, and takes startTime/endTime from session_meta createdAt/updatedAt so the session carries timesEstimated: false and never uses file birthtime/mtime while meta timestamps are present, while a file whose first line is not session_meta degrades to local-cursor behavior (file-time bounds, timesEstimated: true)
  id: ccpp
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'cursor-cloud source preserves cursor-cloud identity|cursor-cloud meta timestamps produce measured start and end times' test/parse.test.js && TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'cursor-cloud file without session_meta falls back to cursor file-time behavior' test/sessions/session-index-cursor-cloud.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, sessions, implemented]
- label: peekSession routes source=cursor-cloud to peekCursorCloud, which consumes the session_meta line (name feeds termFreqs and is the firstPrompt fallback, model seeds the model field before the cursor-cloud fallback) and reuses peekCursor's row handling for the message lines; the peek result carries exactly {firstPrompt, model, termFreqs, tools} per the peek contract — session time bounds are supplied by the indexer/parser from session_meta createdAt/updatedAt (facts ccix, ccpp), never by peek
  id: ccpk
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'cursor-cloud source routes to peekCursorCloud' test/sessions/peek-session-dispatch.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, sessions, implemented]
- label: cursor-cloud snippet scanning and search tokenization reuse the Cursor query-scan over message rows while the session_meta line never yields a snippet, and the source-contract suite covers cursor-cloud like every other source
  id: ccsc
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'cursor-cloud snippets scan message rows and skip the session_meta line|source indexers tokenize source content into per-session termFreqs' test/sessions/scan-queries.test.js test/sessions/search-tokenizer-source-contract.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, search, implemented]
- label: isSessionPath accepts .jsonl files under the resolved cursor-cloud root (default ~/.local/share/tracequest/cursor-cloud or the TRACEQUEST_CURSOR_CLOUD_DIR override) so served session routes can open imported sessions, while files outside the agent roots stay rejected exactly as before
  id: ccsp
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'cursor-cloud paths under the cursor-cloud root are accepted as session paths' test/server/server-session-path.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, serve, implemented]
- label: all three SOURCE_COLORS maps (src/browser/browser-client.js, src/render/render-core-shell.js, src/chapters/compare-metrics.js) gain a dedicated 'cursor-cloud' colour distinct from cursor's, so browser rows, compare cards, and rendered session headers badge cursor-cloud sessions with their own colour
  id: cccl
  command: grep -q 'cursor-cloud' src/browser/browser-client.js && grep -q 'cursor-cloud' src/render/render-core-shell.js && grep -q 'cursor-cloud' src/chapters/compare-metrics.js && node --test --test-name-pattern 'SOURCE_COLORS includes cursor-cloud' test/browser/browser-client.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, ui, implemented]
- label: the filter expression source:cursor-cloud matches exactly the imported cursor-cloud sessions and source:cursor does not match them, because source comparison is exact lowercase equality rather than a prefix match
  id: ccfl
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'source:cursor-cloud matches only cursor-cloud sessions' test/filter-expr.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, search, implemented]

## tests
- label: the incremental-fetch behaviour adds exactly these tests to test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js, each named with the file's existing "import cursor-cloud " prefix — "issues no conversation request for an unchanged agent" (the core regression test: the fixture server's request log must contain the list POST and ZERO conversation POSTs), "refetches an agent whose remote updatedAtMs changed", "fetches an agent that has no local file", "refetches when the local file is corrupt or has no session_meta", "--full refetches every agent including unchanged ones", "fetches conversations concurrently within the bound" (the fixture server tracks maximum simultaneous in-flight conversation requests and asserts it is greater than one and never above CURSOR_CLOUD_FETCH_CONCURRENCY), and "summary reports checked and fetched counts alongside the write outcomes"; tests/cursor-cloud-import-integration.md gains one prose section and one PASS execution-results row per new test and tests/README.md's harness row and scenario totals are updated to match, because the docs-sync test derives those counts from the harness
  id: ccit
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'issues no conversation request for an unchanged agent|refetches an agent whose remote updatedAtMs changed|fetches an agent that has no local file|refetches when the local file is corrupt or has no session_meta|full refetches every agent including unchanged ones|fetches conversations concurrently within the bound|summary reports checked and fetched counts alongside the write outcomes' test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js && TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'tests README scenario totals match package test targets and harness rows|integration spec headers declare matching scenario counts' test/browser/browser-verification-targets.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, cli, implemented]
- label: the import integration test adds these keyless-route tests (the incremental-fetch tests are pinned separately by fact ccit) — "imports keylessly with the local session token", "dedupes the inclusive list pagination boundary by bcId", "maps MESSAGE_TYPE_HUMAN and MESSAGE_TYPE_AI to cursor user and assistant rows", "tool results become tool_use rows that produce tools and toolCounts", "writes session_meta from the keyless list response", "prefers the v0 route when --api-key is given", "dies with an actionable message when no credential can be found", "dies with a re-authenticate message when the local credential is rejected", "skips a keyless conversation that 404s with a warning", "never prints the credential in dry-run or error output", "keyless re-run skips unchanged sessions and preserves mtime", "tolerates unknown status, source, and message type values", and "reads a large single-response conversation without truncation" — each named with the file's existing "import cursor-cloud " prefix
  id: cctn
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'imports keylessly with the local session token|dedupes the inclusive list pagination boundary by bcId|maps MESSAGE_TYPE_HUMAN and MESSAGE_TYPE_AI to cursor user and assistant rows|tool results become tool_use rows that produce tools and toolCounts|writes session_meta from the keyless list response|prefers the v0 route when --api-key is given|dies with an actionable message when no credential can be found|dies with a re-authenticate message when the local credential is rejected|skips a keyless conversation that 404s with a warning|never prints the credential in dry-run or error output|keyless re-run skips unchanged sessions and preserves mtime|tolerates unknown status, source, and message type values|reads a large single-response conversation without truncation' test/bin/tracequest-cursor-cloud-import-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor-cloud, cli, implemented]
